69172a60fcd818" :answer-field/choices ["q-328229/field-4/c43b27454cd69f1b6f4b793e211f4f4f6e40314b87f5b1c35a69172a60fcd818"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-328229" :question/id #uuid "902bf467-1e44-5bf3-9c90-7b9ebb14841b" :question/math-academy-id "q-328229" :question/problem "A  linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation. Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix $T$ of $T?$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=\\frac{5}{3}v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-328229/field-1" "q-328229/field-2" "q-328229/field-3" "q-328229/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "166cd7e4-5568-5769-8bc6-4ffc4ed8b508"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-328229"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
